ty}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/12/122759a459c8dd56dbe69c44c0da4ccffbbc660465fbedc7457e17b42430436e.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n0<{\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}\n$$\n\nthus, ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ is the dominant eigenvalue.\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$  solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector  ${v}_{1}$ as they move away from the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/fc/fcaa3ba63a1e6ad061eecf1f72caf3c9f8ba78b014eff044ee5be682e1325128.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308396 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,0]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/44/44d0f3d13ca03a0644b2f28df9200cd521b7b44d625f346e981d2a54165284f5.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6a/6a4c15275ba80d01a90b2e010ed95e95f9a75cfa13f8e51db3433f6356949057.png)\n\nFinally, since the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-9}},\nsolutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector \n{{field-10}} \nas they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f838a055e525a264bf36b8e0478d186fe17753bba8c0088fea01ac7c5c1f8d7a.png)" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-3x(t)-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,-1]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[1,0]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/44/44d0f3d13ca03a0644b2f28df9200cd521b7b44d625f346e981d2a54165284f5.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6a/6a4c15275ba80d01a90b2e010ed95e95f9a75cfa13f8e51db3433f6356949057.png)\n\nFinally, since the dominant eigenvalue is \n{{field-9}},\nsolutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector \n{{field-10}} \nas they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f838a055e525a264bf36b8e0478d186fe17753bba8c0088fea01ac7c5c1f8d7a.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308396 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/44/44d0f3d13ca03a0644b2f28df9200cd521b7b44d625f346e981d2a54165284f5.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6a/6a4c15275ba80d01a90b2e010ed95e95f9a75cfa13f8e51db3433f6356949057.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}<0\n$$\n\nthus, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue).\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f838a055e525a264bf36b8e0478d186fe17753bba8c0088fea01ac7c5c1f8d7a.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/44/44d0f3d13ca03a0644b2f28df9200cd521b7b44d625f346e981d2a54165284f5.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6a/6a4c15275ba80d01a90b2e010ed95e95f9a75cfa13f8e51db3433f6356949057.png)\n\nFor this particular system, we have\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{2}<{\\lambda }_{1}<0\n$$\n\nthus, ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ is the eigenvalue that determines the long-term behavior (dominant eigenvalue).\n\nSince the dominant eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ solutions become parallel to the straight-line solution containing the vector ${v}_{1}$ as they move towards the origin.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f8/f838a055e525a264bf36b8e0478d186fe17753bba8c0088fea01ac7c5c1f8d7a.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308404 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{6t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/24/2473117cdca4318187b94374820c0a12576e998e7f631a6e7bf51e56c985be43.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{6t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{6{c}_{1}{e}^{6t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/e8/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{6t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{6t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/24/2473117cdca4318187b94374820c0a12576e998e7f631a6e7bf51e56c985be43.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{6t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{4t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{4t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{6t})^{′}}=\\frac{4{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}}{6{c}_{1}{e}^{6t}}=\\frac{2{c}_{2}}{3{c}_{1}}{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as we move away from the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/e8/e8110ea6d6a113e72e35502030afb495a23bc81106d64874582d03aae81b7c73.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308405 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/b1/b1efa82f75d22a304e29adc5a791086bb9d9bf43b2f5c97ba8563f43fd13cc4a.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-2t})^{′}}=\\frac{-3{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}}{-2{c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/a7/a725947c39aba9b8fb866ef66f6ec7d18986a74cb22973f86bab5e2f45816e68.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions also approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](images/b1/b1efa82f75d22a304e29adc5a791086bb9d9bf43b2f5c97ba8563f43fd13cc4a.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions $x(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}$ and $y(t)={c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}$ provided that ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\ne 0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}=\\frac{({c}_{2}{e}^{-3t})^{′}}{({c}_{1}{e}^{-2t})^{′}}=\\frac{-3{c}_{2}{e}^{-3t}}{-2{c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}}=\\frac{3{c}_{2}}{2{c}_{1}}{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](images/a7/a725947c39aba9b8fb866ef66f6ec7d18986a74cb22973f86bab5e2f45816e68.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308409 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $x$-axis and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $x$-axis is the $\\text{stable}$ line.\n\n- Eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=2$ with the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on the $y$-axis and move away from the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, the $y$-axis is the $\\text{unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/8c/8c350c21a6206ec73244dd58601b20a816dcb69557a030099870e2c824aba772.png)" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system o