\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3 \\\\ -{c}_{2}+2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3=7 \\\\ -{c}_{2}+2=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=4.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=4\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]-5\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0}\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0} \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0){e}^{-0}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+3 \\\\ -{c}_{2}+2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}7 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3=7 \\\\ -{c}_{2}+2=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=4.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}-5\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t){e}^{-t} \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t){e}^{-t}\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308481 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a0/a09d50fa36b2748fb1c65ed16e20d950afbbabc18884ff8718cf1571c4e13b0e.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a saddle point}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a0/a09d50fa36b2748fb1c65ed16e20d950afbbabc18884ff8718cf1571c4e13b0e.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308482 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].$" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,x(0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308482 :question/worked-solution "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}-2\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=4 \\\\ {c}_{2}-2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=3.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+3\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We are told that the general solution to the system is \n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot 0)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot 0)\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}{c}_{1}+1 \\\\ {c}_{2}-2\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+1=4 \\\\ {c}_{2}-2=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get ${c}_{1}=3.$ From the second equation, we have ${c}_{2}=3.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\nx(t)=3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+3\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308483 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-2x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/45/450d43a4cbf6dad8d54edbb66fc4a6e44e491290e1dc5d1303057fb0eb7928a7.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-2x$ is $\\text{an unstable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{an unstable node (source)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/45/450d43a4cbf6dad8d54edbb66fc4a6e44e491290e1dc5d1303057fb0eb7928a7.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308485 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a2/a21b978aaab9569ae4dcae346116e51b5248fdf8f3cff461411a2140f70a6a4c.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-x.\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-6t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line and approach the origin as as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, $y=-x$ is $\\text{a stable}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\text{a stable node (sink)}.$ \n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/a2/a21b978aaab9569ae4dcae346116e51b5248fdf8f3cff461411a2140f70a6a4c.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308494 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186308494 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\cdot 5{e}^{-2t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =10{e}^{3t-2t} \\\\  & =10{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2{e}^{3t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5{e}^{-2t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2{e}^{3t}\\cdot 5{e}^{-2t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =10{e}^{3t-2t} \\\\  & =10{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308495 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186308495 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+1 & t \\\\ t & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 1-t\\cdot t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{t}^{2}+1 & t \\\\ t & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)\\cdot 1-t\\cdot t \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1-{t}^{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308497 :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ |  |  |" "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n| ${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$ |  | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | ${e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}$ |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186308497 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{4t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(2t)={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ 2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtraction the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-2t}+{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}) \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-{e}^{4t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}-{e}^{-2t} & {e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308498 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=25x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 5\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},5]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-1}} \\\\ y={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $3.54$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/94/9446137440c19f9aca73c9c666089ecff5a7712ee3f389ab891a150c13ffa780.png)\n\nThe curve is \n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe {{field-8}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/06/0678458809cf39967d7dda0d1ffbe4d43541ec309266bd08da6245ec23f6439d.png)" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=25x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 5\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},5]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-1}} \\\\ y={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $3.54$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/94/9446137440c19f9aca73c9c666089ecff5a7712ee3f389ab