ame{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $5$ | $3.54$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/94/9446137440c19f9aca73c9c666089ecff5a7712ee3f389ab891a150c13ffa780.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)|\\le 1$ and $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)|\\le 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is $\\text{bounded}.$ So, we have $\\text{an ellipse}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/06/0678458809cf39967d7dda0d1ffbe4d43541ec309266bd08da6245ec23f6439d.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308499 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t+1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}t-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}." "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t+1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}t-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe Wronskian of the vector-valued functions given above is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=${{field-1}}."] [:db/cas 17592186308499 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}t+1 & t-1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(t+1)\\cdot 2-(t-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2t+2-2t+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Wronskian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}t+1 & t-1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(t+1)\\cdot 2-(t-1)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2t+2-2t+2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308500 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308500 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{2t} & 0 \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{2t}-0\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{4t} \\\\  & ={e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308501 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-2\\pm 5\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-1}} \\\\ y={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.52$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.52$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/18/18f4793f4f1edc907e28b0e148510dd3c8131a544fb703d91d0a1feb0cd8a4d1.png)\n\nAs $t\\to \\infty ,$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c001f8a99c09e3d1da8c6107a298989c03e64ec58c23319ce0837a757c5ddafd.png)" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-2\\pm 5\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1,2}=[\\pm \\text{i},1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={{field-1}} \\\\ y={{field-2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.52$ | {{field-3}} |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.52$ | {{field-4}} |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/18/18f4793f4f1edc907e28b0e148510dd3c8131a544fb703d91d0a1feb0cd8a4d1.png)\n\nAs $t\\to \\infty ,$ the curve\n{{field-5}}.\nSo, we have\n{{field-6}}\nthat is traversed \n{{field-7}}.\n\nThe spiral is traversed toward \n{{field-8}}\nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c001f8a99c09e3d1da8c6107a298989c03e64ec58c23319ce0837a757c5ddafd.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308501 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ y={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.52$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.52$ | $0.53$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/2b/2b273edc4f977f2bee4d9a8c41128d4f2071ec39b0840610cbb7ee44492aae3e.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)|\\le 1,$ $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)|\\le 1,$ and ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{converges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c001f8a99c09e3d1da8c6107a298989c03e64ec58c23319ce0837a757c5ddafd.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-2t}\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFor example, when ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) \\\\ y={e}^{-2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{20}$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{10}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0.52$ | $0$ |\n| $y$ | $0$ | $0.52$ | $0.53$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](images/2b/2b273edc4f977f2bee4d9a8c41128d4f2071ec39b0840610cbb7ee44492aae3e.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)|\\le 1,$ $|\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)|\\le 1,$ and ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Thus, the curve $\\text{converges}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ So, we have $\\text{a spiral}$ that is traversed $\\text{counterclockwise}.$\n\nAgain, since ${e}^{-2t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ the spiral is traversed toward $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/c0/c001f8a99c09e3d1da8c6107a298989c03e64ec58c23319ce0837a757c5ddafd.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308503 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(2x+y)=4x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one:\n\n$$\n2(2x+y)=4x+2y,\n$$\n\nso we can disregard the second equation.\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -2k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308506 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=0 \\\\ 6x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-3x.$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x+y=0 \\\\ 6x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3x+y=0\\,⇒\\,y=-3x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -3k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-3x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308507 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y=0 \\\\ -2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-x+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{x}{2}.$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-x+2y=0 \\\\ -2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-x+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ \\frac{k}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{x}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308508 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=0 \\\\ 8x-4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 2k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=2x.$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y=0 \\\\ 8x-4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x-2y=0\\,⇒\\,2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ 2k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=2x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308509 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=-x\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{x}{2}.$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y=0 \\\\ 2x+4y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+2y=0\\,⇒\\,2y=-x\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{x}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ -\\frac{k}{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{x}{2}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308510 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=0 \\\\ -6x+8y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-3x+4y=0\\,⇒\\,4y=3x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}k\\end{aligned}],\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{3}{4}x.$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 8\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nThe equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3x+4y=0 \\\\ -6x+8y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the second equation is a multiple of the first one. So, we can disregard the second equation. \n\nFrom first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-3x+4y=0\\,⇒\\,4y=3x\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{3}{4}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}k \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}k\\end{bmatrix},\\,k\\in R.\n$$\n\nThis determines the line with the equation $y=\\frac{3}{4}x.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308515 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=-3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/2b/2bfda58fee727ccdc9f424140e2b8143bf5afe426ef0b1fa72105272d9a1b41e.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/18/18fad31b9f69e55d9f4bc414d3e1b732eaee7685bf36a183033fc302e911eb82.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=-3x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/2b/2bfda58fee727ccdc9f424140e2b8143bf5afe426ef0b1fa72105272d9a1b41e.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/f0/f0821717299fb0b9858409db9a93a610a75e0564a2b60f5bfcd6eca26d2909ec.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308519 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2{e}^{t} \\\\ 6{e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\nwhich is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308519 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t} & 6{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{t})\\cdot (6{e}^{t})-(-2{e}^{t})\\cdot (-3{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}-6{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}{e}^{t} & -2{e}^{t} \\\\ -3{e}^{t} & 6{e}^{t}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =({e}^{t})\\cdot (6{e}^{t})-(-2{e}^{t})\\cdot (-3{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{2t}-6{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{zero for all }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{dependent}$ on $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308520 :question/problem "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}],\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$" "$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix},\\,t\\in R\n$$\n\nConsider the vector-valued functions ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ above. Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions of the same system of linear ODEs, select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ The Wronskian is $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=$ \n{{field-1}},\n\n$\\,$ which is\n{{field-2}}.  \n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly \n{{field-3}} \non $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308520 :question/worked-solution "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=|\\begin{aligned}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$" "The Wronskian $W({x}_{1},{x}_{2})$ of two vector-valued functions \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t)\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{22}(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis defined as\n\n$$\nW({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=det({x}_{1},{x}_{2})=\\begin{vmatrix}{x}_{11}(t) & {x}_{12}(t) \\\\ {x}_{21}(t) & {x}_{22}(t)\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If the Wronskian is not identically zero, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly independent.\n\n- If the Wronskian is identically zero, and it's known that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are solutions to the same system of linear ODEs, then ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are linearly dependent.\n\nIn our case, our Wronskian is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({x}_{1},{x}_{2}) & =\\begin{vmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot (3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =4{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is $\\text{nonzero for at least one }t\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the set ${{x}_{1}(t),{x}_{2}(t)}$ is linearly $\\text{independent}$ on $R.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308528 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186308528 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=3\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{3\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1-\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).$"] [:db/cas 17592186308529 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown function $f(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The matrix has the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ with the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186308529 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nWe are given the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and the corresponding eigenvector ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2+\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\text{i}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $f(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$"] [:db/cas 17592186308530 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fff6a9cacaf140a6f86b498ba9415aef0a4699fcb119146ee49e0c98843eeb.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/34/3499c19ed90a1cbd1916878fe2b9b26401c4eedd656cb9026fbdf35e5b78acb4.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/12/126db28b161fa823ae5048f79f0ed4516cf3b24053f83cbcd9bc564ae11cd3ef.png)" "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/41/41fff6a9cacaf140a6f86b498ba9415aef0a4699fcb119146ee49e0c98843eeb.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=\\frac{1}{2}x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{4t}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{4t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach infinity as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/34/3499c19ed90a1cbd1916878fe2b9b26401c4eedd656cb9026fbdf35e5b78acb4.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/12/126db28b161fa823ae5048f79f0ed4516cf3b24053f83cbcd9bc564ae11cd3ef.png)"] [:db/cas 17592186308531 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}f(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}g(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$*\n\n$f(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,g(t)=$\n{{field-2}}" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above.\nFind the unknown functions $f(t)$ and $g(t)$ in the general solution to the system, shown below.\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}f(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}g(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n*Hint: One of the eigenvalues of the matrix is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$*\n\n$f(t)=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,g(t)=$\n{{field-2}}"] [:db/cas 17592186308531 :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-12\\text{i} & 18 \\\\ -8 & -12\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{12i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{\\text{12i}t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]+\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t),\\,g(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t).\n$$" "We need to find the eigenvalues and associated eigenvectors of the matrix \n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 18 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}.$ \n\nLet's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors. For ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i},$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=\\begin{bmatrix}-12\\text{i} & 18 \\\\ -8 & -12\\text{i}\\end{bmatrix}∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3\\text{i} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Note:** The eigenvector corresponding to the second eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\overset{―}{\\lambda }}_{1}$ is \n${v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}.$ But we do not require this eigenvector for solving our system.\n\nThe solution that we obtain for the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=12\\text{i}$ and its associated eigenvector is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{12i}t}.\n$$\n\nWe now express this as a real function. Using Euler's formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{\\text{12i}t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)-3\\text{i}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)+2\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\text{i}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+\\text{i}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{2}$ are linearly independent. Therefore, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & ={c}_{1}{\\text{w}}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{\\text{w}}_{2}(t) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nf(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12t),\\,g(t)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(12t).\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308532 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-4$ and $0.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-3+3{e}^{-4t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $3-{e}^{-4t}$ |  |  |" "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-4$ and $0.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}$ |  | {{field-1}} | $-3+3{e}^{-4t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $3-{e}^{-4t}$ |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186308532 :question/worked-solution "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t)={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{-4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-6(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+3{e}^{-4t}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+3{e}^{-4t}}{2} \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1+3{e}^{-4t} & -3+3{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-4t} & 3-{e}^{-4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "We have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & 2{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-4t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-4t)={e}^{-4t} \\\\ r(0)=1\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-4t} \\\\ {\\alpha }_{0}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- From the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Substituting ${\\alpha }_{0}=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n-4t{\\alpha }_{1}+1={e}^{-4t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =-6(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1)+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+3{e}^{-4t}+2}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1+3{e}^{-4t}}{2} \\\\ 2{\\alpha }_{1}t & =2(-\\frac{1}{4t}({e}^{-4t}-1))t \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{4}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-4t}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1-{e}^{-4t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}-1+3{e}^{-4t} & -3+3{e}^{-4t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-4t} & 3-{e}^{-4t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308533 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-2$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | $-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ | $-2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of $A$ are $-2$ and $1.$*\n\n| ${e}^{At}=\\,\\,$ |  | $-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ | $-2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t}$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186308533 :question/worked-solution "Explanation:\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]t+{\\alpha }_{0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =-({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((-4{e}^{-2t}+4{e}^{t})+({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} & -2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "Explanation:\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & -6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 3{\\alpha }_{1}t & 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are functions to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-2t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=t,\n$$\n\nboth of multiplicity one. Thus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}r(-2t)={e}^{-2t} \\\\ r(t)={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,{\\begin{aligned}-2t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t} \\\\ t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-3t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\n- Multiplying the second equation by $2$ and adding to the first one, we get\n\n$$\n3{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\alpha }_{1}t & =3(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t \\\\  & =-({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} \\\\ 4{\\alpha }_{1}t+{\\alpha }_{0} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3t}({e}^{-2t}-{e}^{t}))t+\\frac{1}{3}({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}((-4{e}^{-2t}+4{e}^{t})+({e}^{-2t}+2{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}-{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} & -2{e}^{t}+2{e}^{-2t} \\\\ {e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t} & 2{e}^{t}-{e}^{-2t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186308537 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150px\">\n{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"150px\">\n{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186308537 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t