e that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At}\\,x(0) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & 