nd{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186310641 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3{e}^{-5t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-3{e}^{-5t} \\\\ -3t{e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186310642 :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}]" "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [:db/cas 17592186310725 :answer/value "$[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]$ only" "$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186311546 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=3u(t)-v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311547 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-u(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311548 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {u}^{′} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}](u+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]u+[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nTo shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x$ and ${x}^{′}$ into the given system, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}x+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}(u+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u+\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+5v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311559 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186311559 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\, \\\\ \\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\, \\\\ \\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311560 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-7y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-7y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186311560 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\, \\\\ \\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)\\, \\\\ \\,-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7t)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7t)\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311561 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186311561 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(7t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(7t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311562 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=6y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186311562 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4t{e}^{6t} \\\\ -4{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t} & t{e}^{6t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4t{e}^{6t} \\\\ -4{e}^{6t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311563 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-5y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186311563 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-5t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,x(0),\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system, and $x(0)$ is the initial condition vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-5t} & t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3t{e}^{-5t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-5t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311564 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n| $[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=3y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & t{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186311564 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-2)}\\,x(2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3(t-2)} & (t-2){e}^{3(t-2)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-2)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,(t-2){e}^{3(t-2)}\\, \\\\ \\,{e}^{3(t-2)}\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-2)}\\,x(2) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3(t-2)} & (t-2){e}^{3(t-2)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3(t-2)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,(t-2){e}^{3(t-2)}\\, \\\\ \\,{e}^{3(t-2)}\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311578 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+(v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1+v(t)+1-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+3(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =2u(t)+2-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+3v(t)+3-4 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+(v(t)+1)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1+v(t)+1-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+3(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =2u(t)+2-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+3v(t)+3-4 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311580 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-x(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+(v(t)+2)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2u(t)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+3(v(t)+2)-2 \\\\  & =2u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+3v(t)+6-2 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t),\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-x(t)+y(t)-2 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+(v(t)+2)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+3y(t)-2 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2u(t)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+3(v(t)+2)-2 \\\\  & =2u(t)-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+3v(t)+6-2 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)-v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311581 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+3(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+1)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+3u(t)-3+v(t)+1+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-3(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+2(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+2v(t)+2-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+1,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+3x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+3(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+1)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+3u(t)-3+v(t)+1+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =-3x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+2y(t)-4 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =-3(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+1)^{2}+2(v(t)+1)-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)+3-({v}^{2}(t)+2v(t)+1)+2v(t)+2-4 \\\\  & =-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=-3u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311582 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t)-3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+(v(t)+2)^{2}-3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)-3u(t)-3-v(t)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-8 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+5(v(t)+2)-8 \\\\  & =2u(t)+2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+5v(t)+10-8 \\\\  & =2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t)\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+{y}^{2}(t)-3x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+1)^{2}+(v(t)+2)^{2}-3(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2) \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+1)+({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)-3u(t)-3-v(t)-2 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+5y(t)-8 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)+1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+5(v(t)+2)-8 \\\\  & =2u(t)+2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+5v(t)+10-8 \\\\  & =2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)-u(t)+{v}^{2}(t)+3v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)+v(t)-{v}^{2}(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311583 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+4x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+4(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+2)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+4u(t)-4+v(t)+2+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+8y(t)-10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+8(v(t)+2)-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+8v(t)+16-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)-1,\\,y(t)=v(t)+2,\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)+4x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)-1)^{2}+4(u(t)-1)+(v(t)+2)+1 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)-2u(t)+1)+4u(t)-4+v(t)+2+1 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =2x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+8y(t)-10 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =2(u(t)-1)-(v(t)+2)^{2}+8(v(t)+2)-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-2-({v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)+4)+8v(t)+16-10 \\\\  & =2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2u(t)+v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=2u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+4v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311590 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(4) \\\\ y(4)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & t{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"100px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(4) \\\\ y(4)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & t{e}^{t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"100px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186311590 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-4)}\\,x(4) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t-4} & (t-4){e}^{t-4} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t-4}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,(5-t){e}^{t-4}\\, \\\\ \\,-{e}^{t-4}\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-4)}\\,x(4) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t-4} & (t-4){e}^{t-4} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t-4}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,(5-t){e}^{t-4}\\, \\\\ \\,-{e}^{t-4}\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311591 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-3) \\\\ y(-3)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7t} & t{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=7x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=7y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(-3) \\\\ y(-3)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{7t} & t{e}^{7t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7t}\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"140px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186311591 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+3)}\\,x(-3) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{7(t+3)} & (t+3){e}^{7(t+3)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7(t+3)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,(-2t-6){e}^{7(t+3)}\\, \\\\ \\,-2{e}^{7(t+3)}\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+3)}\\,x(-3) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{7(t+3)} & (t+3){e}^{7(t+3)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{7(t+3)}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,(-2t-6){e}^{7(t+3)}\\, \\\\ \\,-2{e}^{7(t+3)}\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311592 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-1) \\\\ y(-1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(-1) \\\\ y(-1)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [:db/cas 17592186311592 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+1)}\\,x(-1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8(t+1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8(t+1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8(t+1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8(t+1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8(t+1))\\, \\\\ \\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8(t+1))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+1)}\\,x(-1) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8(t+1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8(t+1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8(t+1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8(t+1))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8(t+1))\\, \\\\ \\,5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8(t+1))\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311593 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(2) \\\\ y(2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186311593 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-2)}\\,x(2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-2)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-2)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-2)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-2))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-2)}\\,x(2) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-2)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-2)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-2))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(5(t-2)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(5(t-2))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311594 :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix},$ where $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$*"] [:db/cas 17592186311594 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have \n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-(-2))}\\,x(-2) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have \n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c,\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-(-2))}\\,x(-2) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2(t+2)) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2(t+2))\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311602 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-1=0 \\\\ 2x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding both equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n3x-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+2y-1=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+2y-1=0 \\\\ 2x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding both equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n3x-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}+2y-1=0\\,⇒\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311604 :question/worked-solution "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $u=[\\begin{aligned}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{aligned}].$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,⇒\\,x=u+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+3,\\,y(t)=v(t),\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-6x(t)+2y(t)+9 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+3)^{2}-6(u(t)+3)+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+6u(t)+9)-6u(t)-18+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+y(t)-12 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =4(u(t)+3)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+12-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "To shift the system's equilibrium from ${x}^{∗}$ to the origin, we define the deviation vector\n\n$$\nu=x-{x}^{∗},\n$$\n\nwhere $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$ and $u=\\begin{bmatrix}u(t) \\\\ v(t)\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nu=x-\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,x=u+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}={u}^{′}.\n$$\n\nIn coordinate form, this can be written as follows:\n\n$$\nx(t)=u(t)+3,\\,y(t)=v(t),\\,{x}^{′}(t)={u}^{′}(t),\\,{v}^{′}(t)={y}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nLet's now substitute these into the given system.\n\n- Substituting into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & ={x}^{2}(t)-6x(t)+2y(t)+9 \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & =(u(t)+3)^{2}-6(u(t)+3)+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & =({u}^{2}(t)+6u(t)+9)-6u(t)-18+2v(t)+9 \\\\  & ={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(t) & =4x(t)-{y}^{2}(t)+y(t)-12 \\\\ {v}^{′}(t) & =4(u(t)+3)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)+12-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)-12 \\\\  & =4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shifted system is the following:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′}(t)={u}^{2}(t)+2v(t) \\\\ {v}^{′}(t)=4u(t)-{v}^{2}(t)+v(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311613 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ x(4x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\n- For $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get $y=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y=0\\,⇒\\,y=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ x(4x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\text{ }\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=2\\cdot 0=0.$\n\n- For $x=\\frac{1}{4},$ we get $y=2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311615 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=0 \\\\ x+y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-3y=0 \\\\ x+y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-5y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\nx+0=0\\,⇒\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311616 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y-4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2y-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2(-x-2)-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+4-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\text{ }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-2=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=-(-2)-2=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2y-4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y+2=0\\,⇒\\,y=-x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $y$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2y-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2(-x-2)-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+4-4 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,\\text{ }0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $y:$\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get $y=-0-2=-2.$\n\n- For $x=-2,$ we get $y=-(-2)-2=0.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311617 :question/worked-solution "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+x-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+x-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+(1-y)-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,\\text{ }0,\\text{ }1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $x:$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=1-0=1.$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get $x=1-1=0.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get $x=1-(-1)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,\\text{and}\\,[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+x-1=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the first equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y-1=0\\,⇒\\,x=1-y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x$ into the second equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}+x-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}+(1-y)-1 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{3}-y & =0 \\\\ y({y}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ y(y-1)(y+1) & =0 \\\\ y & =-1,\\text{ }0,\\text{ }1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $x:$\n\n- For $y=0,$ we get $x=1-0=1.$\n\n- For $y=1,$ we get $x=1-1=0.$\n\n- For $y=-1,$ we get $x=1-(-1)=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium points are\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\text{and}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311651 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-1,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$x\\,-\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-1,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$x\\,-\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$y\\,+$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186311664 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,3,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n$3x\\,-\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$y\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$=0$" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(-2,3,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n$3x\\,-\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$y\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,+$\n{{field-3}}\n\n$=0$"] [:db/cas 17592186311692 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-4,3,5⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =36i+16j+4k \\\\  & =⟨36,16,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =⟨-4,3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z+76 & =0 \\\\ 9x+4y+z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-4,3,5⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -8\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =36i+16j+4k \\\\  & =⟨36,16,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =⟨-4,3,5⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨36,16,4⟩ & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z & =-76 \\\\ 36x+16y+4z+76 & =0 \\\\ 9x+4y+z+19 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311695 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,p=⟨3,4,2⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,2,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,3⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =0i-8j+8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-8,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =⟨3,4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z+16 & =0 \\\\ y-z-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,1,-1⟩,\\,p=⟨3,4,2⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,2,0⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,4,2⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3,3⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,0⟩-⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,1,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =0i-8j+8k \\\\  & =⟨0,-8,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =⟨3,4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,-8,8⟩ & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z & =-16 \\\\ -8y+8z+16 & =0 \\\\ y-z-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311696 :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-2,1⟩,\\,p=⟨5,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,3⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-10i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨-10,4,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =⟨5,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17 \\\\ -10x+4y+11z & =-17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z & =17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z-17 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨2,-2,1⟩,\\,p=⟨5,0,3⟩,$ and $q=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨5,0,3⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,2,2⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,-1⟩-⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-10i+4j+11k \\\\  & =⟨-10,4,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =⟨5,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-10,4,11⟩ & =-17 \\\\ -10x+4y+11z & =-17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z & =17 \\\\ 10x-4y-11z-17 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311697 :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-3,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+t[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$" "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(2,-3,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$r=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186311708 :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,-4,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =11i+1j+4k \\\\  & =⟨11,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =⟨2,-4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z-30 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨2,-4,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =11i+1j+4k \\\\  & =⟨11,1,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =⟨2,-4,3⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨11,1,4⟩ & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z & =30 \\\\ 11x+y+4z-30 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311737 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]}},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $5{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |" "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+\\overset{f(t)}{\\overset{⏞}{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}}},\\,Φ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n| ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $5{e}^{-t}$ |  | $\\,,\\,$ | ${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | $-4$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-1}} |  |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186311737 :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-({e}^{3t})\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -3{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix},\\,f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{3t}-({e}^{3t})\\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4t}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{3t} & -{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{-t} & -{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\begin{bmatrix}5{e}^{-t} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\int 5{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -3{e}^{-3t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{t} & {e}^{3t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{3t}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-5{e}^{-t} \\\\ {e}^{-3t}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186311821 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 